## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Animals in Our Waters</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Receptive: Analyze the tone and purpose of spoken advertisements. Interactive: Participate in group problem-solving sessions.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can analyze the tone and purpose in Public Service Announcements (PSAs) from local organizations about marine conservation (e.g., "Identify if a PSA about the Panama Bay wetlands by MarViva is persuasive or informative." "Determine if a government announcement about protecting mangroves is raising awareness or promoting action.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can ask questions to clarify information on marine life (e.g., "Why is it important to protect coral reefs from pollution?" "Reefs provide a habitat for a lot of ocean life.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can participate in group problem-solving discussions by sharing actionable ideas to protect Panama's oceans and coastal ecosystems (e.g., "Suggest organizing a mangrove replanting day in Juan Diz to reduce erosion and protect marine life." "Propose creating an educational campaign in schools to teach students about avoiding single-use plastics." "Recommend participating in a beach cleanup at Isla Taboga to reduce plastic waste and raise awareness about marine conservation.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows images or a short video (1 minute) of diverse ocean animals and plants (e.g., coral reefs, fish, sharks, mangroves). Students brainstorm what they know about "The Animals in Our Waters" and share 1-2 words/phrases they associate with the images. The teacher writes their ideas on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words by displaying pictures and pronouncing each word clearly, emphasizing stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "ocean," "ecosystem," "biodiversity," "endangered," "conservation," "pollution," "habitat," "marine life," "coral reefs," "mangroves," "Public Service Announcement (PSA)"). The teacher models a short sentence for each word (e.g., "The <b>ocean</b> is vast." "Many <b>species</b> live in <b>coral reefs</b>." "Plastic is a big problem for <b>pollution</b>."). Students repeat chorally and individually, focusing on correct pronunciation, including minimal pairs like /iː/ in "reef" and /ɪ/ in "riff" if applicable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is pollution? Is it good or bad for the ocean?" (Expected: "Bad.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If an animal is 'endangered,' what does that mean for its survival?" (Expected: "It's at risk, it needs protection.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic audio recording of a short (approx. 1:30 min) Public Service Announcement (PSA) from a local Panamanian organization (e.g., MarViva or ANAM) about protecting Panama Bay's coastal areas, marine life, and mangroves from pollution. The register is informative and persuasive.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Comprehending the main ideas and specific details in spoken texts related to environmental conservation, specifically identifying the purpose and tone of PSAs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the PSA. After listening, they answer: "What is the main topic of this Public Service Announcement? Is it about cars, trees, or the ocean?" (Expected: "The ocean and its animals/plants.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and identify specific details. The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements based on the PSA. Examples:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The PSA talks about protecting coral reefs." (True/False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It mentions that pollution is good for marine life." (True/False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The PSA suggests that we should conserve our coastal areas." (True/False)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Matching Purpose/Tone):</b> Students listen one more time. The teacher asks: "Is this PSA mostly (A) giving information or (B) asking people to do something?" Students match the PSA's purpose (informative/persuasive) and tone (serious/hopeful) from a given list of options on the board.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific vocabulary and grammatical structures (Present simple, Present continuous, Modals for suggestions and obligations) in context of marine conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher plays short excerpts (1-2 sentences) from the PSA or similar audio. Students have a worksheet with sentences from the audio, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., "pollution," "habitat," "protect," "endangered," "conservation," "mangroves") missing. They listen and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Plastic ________ is harming our marine animals."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Coral reefs provide a vital ________ for many species."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We ________ to work together to ________ our oceans."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Type Identification):</b> The teacher plays 4-5 new short audio clips (10-15 seconds each) related to ocean life. For each clip, students identify if the speaker is primarily:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(A) Describing ocean life/an ongoing action (using Present Simple/Continuous, e.g., "The sharks are swimming... Coral reefs are home...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(B) Suggesting or obligating an action (using Modals, e.g., "We should protect... We must conserve...")</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students write A or B next to each number. The teacher reviews answers, asking students to explain their choices based on keywords they heard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Summarize):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays two different, very short (20-30 seconds) audio clips of PSAs or announcements about marine life problems (e.g., overfishing, plastic waste). After each clip, pairs discuss and summarize: "What is the problem in this clip?" and "Is the speaker mostly informative or persuasive?" They must use at least two new vocabulary words and one modal verb in their summary. (e.g., "The clip is about ocean pollution. We should stop littering near oceans to protect marine animals.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group problem-solving discussion, followed by a brief presentation of solutions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Scenario Introduction:</b> The teacher presents a new audio clip (approx. 1 minute) of a local news report or a simplified interview about a specific marine conservation challenge in Panama (e.g., plastic pollution on Isla Taboga, destruction of mangroves in Juan Díaz, impact on endangered species in Coiba).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Group Problem-Solving:</b> In groups of 3-4, students listen to the audio carefully. After listening, they discuss the problem identified in the audio. Their task is to brainstorm and propose 2-3 actionable ideas to address this problem in Panama's oceans or coastal ecosystems. Students must actively participate in the discussion, sharing ideas and asking clarifying questions using target vocabulary and modals for suggestions ("We should organize...", "What about creating...?", "We need to protect...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Presenting Solutions:</b> Each group selects one member to briefly present their top two solutions to the class. The presenter clearly states the problem (from the audio) and their group's proposed actions. (e.g., "The news report talks about pollution near Isla Taboga. We should organize a beach cleanup there to reduce plastic waste and raise awareness about marine conservation.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing environmental issues, collaborative interaction, applying pragmatic functions (proposing conservation actions), and using sociolinguistic competence (providing suggestions during discussions on marine protection).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups propose 2-3 actionable ideas for marine conservation after listening to an audio clip detailing a specific problem. Teacher observes group discussions and listens to group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., "ecosystem," "endangered," "conservation," "mangroves").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., "pollution," "habitat," "protect," "species").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of modal verbs for suggestions/obligations (e.g., "We should...", "We must...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and relevance of proposed conservation actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative participation in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection on a slip of paper.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned today is _______. Its meaning is _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it (easy/difficult) to understand the main idea of the PSAs because _______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one important thing you learned today about protecting our oceans?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use the new vocabulary or phrases from today's lesson outside of class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to achieve the listening objectives? The varied audio inputs (PSA, news report) kept engagement high, and the focus on purpose/tone was successfully introduced.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students struggled with distinguishing between informative and persuasive tone. More explicit examples or a visual aid might be helpful for future listening tasks. Reinforce listening for signal words for suggestions in the next lesson.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about marine life and conservation, reinforcing the vocabulary introduced today and building on their understanding of the topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>